(7357471, 1150686, 123833, '<td valign="top">Thanks for picking my image. Do you live in Italy? I am going to Venice again in October and November and hope they will finish renovations!</td>', 'Thanks for picking my image. Do you live in Italy? I am going to Venice again in October and November and hope they will finish renovations!', '2015-06-05 08:47:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(7357472, 1150749, 49109, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by BrennanOB:</b></div><hr/><i> Oliver is perhaps the best guy with studio light on the site, so taking on one of his images is always a challenge, and for a shot like this the details are key.  </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks, Brennan, I feel honored!</td>', 'Originally posted by BrennanOB: Oliver is perhaps the best guy with studio light on the site, so taking on one of his images is always a challenge, and for a shot like this the details are key.    Thanks, Brennan, I feel honored!', '2015-06-05 08:59:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(7357508, 1150964, 58667, '<td valign="top">I like this and the idea, so I''d like to try give some advice (maybe by writing my thoughts I can learn something). I think the original''s background and lighting are the first major difference. Also the perspective. So experiment with that. The lighting on yours is quite harsh, so the reflections are quite hot when bumping the contrast. I think (don''t know) that soft light from a diffused source works here - also the BG needs to be well lit.<br/> <br/>Then, for post, you can increase the contrast and correct the colours. I think if you''ve set up light properly, post is easy. I''ve found that using duplicates of the image with different blending modes give nice contrasts.<br/> <br/>I''d love to have time to experiment with bubbles like this because I think I might be able to learn a lot in the process.</td>', 'I like this and the idea, so I''d like to try give some advice (maybe by writing my thoughts I can learn something). I think the original''s background and lighting are the first major difference. Also the perspective. So experiment with that. The lighting on yours is quite harsh, so the reflections are quite hot when bumping the contrast. I think (don''t know) that soft light from a diffused source works here - also the BG needs to be well lit. Then, for post, you can increase the contrast and correct the colours. I think if you''ve set up light properly, post is easy. I''ve found that using duplicates of the image with different blending modes give nice contrasts. I''d love to have time to experiment with bubbles like this because I think I might be able to learn a lot in the process.', '2015-06-05 10:31:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(7357535, 1151051, 86447, '<td valign="top">Always happens to me, too. <br/> <br/>Anyway, I love your take on this.   Made me smile.<br/> <br/>Still does.  :D<br/></td>', 'Always happens to me, too.  Anyway, I love your take on this.   Made me smile. Still does.  :D', '2015-06-05 10:48:33.000000', NULL, 0),
